==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|en|envoke||archaic|or|misspelling}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*wekʷ-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|*invoken}}, {{m|enm|envoken}}, borrowed from {{der|en|fro|envoquer}}, from {{der|en|la|invocō|invocāre|to call upon}}, itself from {{prefix|la|in-|vocare|t2=to call|nocat=1}}. {{doublet|en|invocate}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|in'vōk}}
* {{IPA|en|/ɪnˈvoʊk/|a=GA}}
* {{IPA|en|/ɪnˈvəʊk/|a=RP}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-invoke.wav|a=Southern England}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[call]] upon (a [[person]], a [[god]]) for [[help]], [[assistance]] or [[guidance]].
#: {{syn|en|invocate|q1=obsolete}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[solicit]], petition for, [[appeal]] to a favorable attitude.
#: {{syn|en|solicit|appeal|petition}}
#: {{ux|en|The envoy '''invoked''' the King of Kings's magnanimity to reduce his province's tribute after another drought.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|nautical|of one ship}} To call another ship.
#: {{syn|en|signal}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To call to mind (something) for some purpose.
#: {{syn|en|evoke|bring to mind|call to mind|enmind}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[appeal]] for validation to a (notably cited) [[authority]].
#: {{syn|en|cite|reference|appeal}}
#: {{ux|en|In certain Christian circles, '''invoking''' the Bible constitutes irrefutable proof.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1969|author=Philip Ziegler|title=The Black Death|page=21|publisher=Folio Society|year_published=2007
|passage=He '''invoked''' cadaveric poisoning as the reason for the high death rate among priests and monks {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Guardian|date=2016-03-03|author=Jack Shenker|title=Welcome to the land that no country wants|url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2016/mar/03/welcome-to-the-land-that-no-country-wants-bir-tawil|passage=Their ties to the area may be based on traditional rather than written claims – but Bir Tawil is not any more a “no man’s land” than the territory once known as British East Africa, where ''terra nullius'' was repeatedly '''invoked''' in the early 20th century by both chartered companies and the British government that supported them to justify the appropriation of territory from indigenous people.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20200406155850/https://www.theguardian.com/world/2016/mar/03/welcome-to-the-land-that-no-country-wants-bir-tawil}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[conjure]] up with [[incantation]]s.
#: {{syn|en|summon}}
#: {{ux|en|This satanist ritual '''invokes''' Beelzebub.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[bring about]] as an inevitable consequence.
#: {{syn|en|bring about|incite|Thesaurus:incite}}
#: {{ux|en|Blasphemy is taboo as it may '''invoke''' divine wrath.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|computing}} To cause (a [[program]] or [[subroutine]]) to execute.
#: {{syn|en|call|execute|launch|run}}
#: {{ux|en|Interactive programs let the users enter choices and '''invoke''' the corresponding routines.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=August 7, 1974|journal=Computerworld|title=Wholesalers Gain Basic Facts|titleurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=yeSH1uyhLwQC&pg=PA13&lpg=PA12|passage={{...}}the selling price and cost of a particular item are derived by the system through a table lookup and assigned to the item at '''invoking''' time.}}

